    <section id="outdoor-safety-location-disclaimer">
      <h2>Outdoor Safety and Location Disclaimer</h2>
      <p>Red River Gorge Hiker contains photographs, stories, descriptions, observations, route information, maps, GPS tracks, GPX downloads, and general information concerning outdoor locations, including backcountry and off-trail areas. Routes &amp; Tracks information is provided for general information and trip planning under the additional terms below and is not a live navigation service, emergency or rescue service, land survey, title opinion, legal-access determination, or substitute for current information from the appropriate land-management agency or other responsible authority.</p>
      <p>Hiking, backpacking, climbing, scrambling, creek travel, off-trail exploration, and other backcountry activities involve inherent risks, including difficult terrain, cliffs, falling rock, changing weather, flooding, high water, falling trees and limbs, wildlife, navigation errors, inaccessible routes, injury, and death. Conditions can change substantially after photographs are taken or information is published.</p>
      <p>You are solely responsible for determining whether a location can be legally and safely accessed, evaluating current conditions, obtaining any required permits or permissions, selecting appropriate equipment, and determining whether an activity is within your abilities. Always follow applicable laws, closures, regulations, private-property boundaries, posted restrictions, and guidance from the relevant land-management authorities.</p>
      <p>Any decision to visit, locate, approach, or attempt to access a place described or depicted on this website is made voluntarily and at your own risk. Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC, operating under the Red River Gorge Hiker brand, makes no representation or warranty that any location shown or discussed on the website is currently accessible, publicly accessible, safe, accurately described, or suitable for any particular visitor.</p>
      <p>To the fullest extent permitted by applicable law, Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC shall not be responsible for injuries, losses, damages, expenses, or other consequences arising from a visitor's use of or reliance upon outdoor, geographic, historical, safety, access, or location-related information provided through this website.</p>
      <p>Detailed off-trail directions, sensitive locations, coordinates, or access information may be withheld at the discretion of Red River Gorge Hiker.</p>
      <p>Interactive-map location controls may use the location reported by your browser or device after you grant permission. GPS and device-location estimates can be inaccurate, delayed, or unavailable, especially around cliffs, hollows, tree cover, poor satellite geometry, or limited connectivity. A displayed device location is not a substitute for a dedicated backcountry navigation method, an offline map, judgment, or current land-manager information.</p>
      <p>Property and parcel boundaries are not displayed on the RRGH interactive map. Map lines, routes, trails, roads, recreation sites, informal paths, management areas, and device-location markers do not establish ownership, legal access, permission, current opening status, or a right to enter or travel through any place.</p>
      <p>The optional Sunrise / Sunset potential layer is a generalized high-ground terrain and mapped-water model intended as a photography-planning aid. It excludes mapped water, strongly suppresses low valley terrain, and uses relative elevation, local relief, ridge/upper-shoulder prominence, convexity, slope/aspect, and directional terrain-horizon clearance across representative seasonal sunrise and sunset directions. Its colors do not guarantee that the sun will be visible from a location or that a location will provide a good view. Weather, haze, vegetation, buildings, seasonal foliage, access restrictions, and smaller local obstructions may materially change actual conditions.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Search &amp; Rescue and Emergency Information</h2>
      <p>Red River Gorge Hiker is not an emergency service, search-and-rescue agency, official safety authority, or dispatch service. If you are experiencing an active emergency, contact 911 or the appropriate public emergency authority rather than attempting to contact Red River Gorge Hiker through this website.</p>
      <p>The Search &amp; Rescue section may summarize general preparedness practices and publicly available information from land-management, weather, transportation, park, or emergency organizations. Rules, closures, access, weather, road conditions, permits, and emergency circumstances can change. Visitors should confirm current information directly with the linked responsible agency before a trip and follow emergency-dispatch instructions during an incident.</p>
      <p>Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC independently supports Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue. RRGH maintains a minimum $500 annual Company commitment and separately allocates 20% of positive Red River Gorge Hiker business profit. These commitments are separate and additive; neither offsets nor satisfies the other. Historical personal support by Ryan D. Lewis remains separate from Company support.</p>
      <p>No formal partnership, sponsorship, endorsement, agency relationship, promotional arrangement, or commercial relationship with Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue is stated or implied. Neither Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC nor Ryan D. Lewis speaks for Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue.</p>
      <p>Links inviting visitors to donate directly to Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue send visitors to WCSART's own public donation system. Direct charitable donations do not pass through Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC or Ryan D. Lewis, and neither Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC nor Ryan D. Lewis processes, holds, or relays those direct donations.</p>
    </section>


    <section id="routes-maps-gpx">
      <h2>Routes, Maps, GPS Tracks, and Location Information</h2>
      <p>Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC (“RRGH”) may publish route descriptions, maps, GPS tracks, downloadable GPX files, coordinates, elevation profiles, photographs, land-management information, and other location information documenting routes that have previously been traveled, researched, compiled, or reviewed. RRGH may include official trails, established unofficial routes, selected off-trail routes, roads, access points, crossings, public-land or management boundaries, and other geographic information.</p>
      <p>This information is provided for general information and trip planning. It is not a live navigation service, emergency or rescue service, land survey, title opinion, legal-access determination, or guarantee that any route, trail, road, access point, crossing, boundary, destination, or other location is currently open, legal, accurate, safe, passable, or suitable for you or your trip. Publication of a route does not mean RRGH recommends that every visitor attempt it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Outdoor and Backcountry Risk; User Responsibility</h2>
      <p>Hiking, backpacking, scrambling, road travel, water crossings, and off-trail travel can involve serious hazards and may result in property damage, getting lost, delayed rescue, serious injury, or death. Hazards may include cliffs and falls, unstable rock or soil, steep or rough terrain, trees and blowdowns, high or swift water, flash flooding, weather, heat or cold, wildlife, poor visibility, remote terrain, limited or absent cellular service, road hazards, and conditions that are not identified on a map or route page.</p>
      <p>You are responsible for deciding whether, when, where, and how to travel. You are responsible for your own preparation, physical condition, skills, equipment, navigation, weather and water-level awareness, emergency planning, party management, permissions, and go/no-go decisions. You should be prepared to turn around or change plans when conditions, access, your equipment, your abilities, or your judgment do not support continuing.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Conditions, Closures, and Navigation</h2>
      <p>Routes and conditions change. Trails and roads may be rerouted, damaged, obscured, closed, gated, flooded, washed out, eroded, blocked by trees, affected by fire or storm damage, or subject to new rules after RRGH last traveled or reviewed them. Water levels, weather, seasonal vegetation, parking, road conditions, bridge or crossing conditions, and land-manager rules may change quickly.</p>
      <p>GPS devices, phones, satellite receivers, digital maps, elevation models, aerial imagery, GPX files, and other geographic data can contain errors, offsets, gaps, stale information, or differences in accuracy. A GPX line may show a previously traveled or compiled path and does not mean there is a maintained trail, visible tread, legal right-of-way, or safe passage at every point. Do not rely on RRGH content or any single electronic device as your only navigation or safety resource. Verify current conditions, closures, rules, and access with the applicable land manager or other authoritative source before departure and as conditions require.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Property, Boundaries, and Access</h2>
      <p>Any parcel, ownership, public-land, Wilderness, administrative, land-management, or similar boundary shown by RRGH is approximate and informational. It is not a survey, deed, title record, legal description, boundary determination, right-of-way determination, or legal opinion. A mapped line or route does not establish ownership, permission, public access, or a legal right to enter or cross property.</p>
      <p>You are responsible for confirming current legal access, permits, closures, restrictions, and property-owner or land-manager permission. Do not trespass. When authoritative current access information conflicts with RRGH content, the authoritative current information controls. RRGH may withhold, generalize, change, or remove route or location information when access, resource protection, safety, sensitivity, or legal status warrants doing so.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Off-Trail and Unmaintained Routes</h2>
      <p>Some RRGH routes may intentionally include travel away from maintained trails or on faint, unofficial, abandoned, or unmaintained paths. These routes may have no signs, blazes, maintained tread, bridges, or obvious route to follow and may require substantially greater navigation judgment, terrain assessment, self-reliance, and turnaround discipline. They may also present greater risk of cliffs, unstable terrain, vegetation, water crossings, resource damage, and delayed rescue.</p>
      <p>Publication of an off-trail route is not an instruction to create a new trail, cut vegetation, build cairns, disturb cultural or natural resources, enter a closure, or cross private property. Route-specific warnings and withholding decisions may be more restrictive than these general Terms.</p>
    </section>

